Pull the skeleton out of the situation below: the structure that any analogy for it has to
copy. Later steps draft analogies from this skeleton and check them against it, so a guess or a
gap here turns into an analogy that sounds right and points the wrong way.

<summary>
{{.Summary}}
</summary>

<audience>
{{.Audience}}
</audience>

Stay with what the summary says or plainly implies. If it never states the decision, the
point, or the cost of doing nothing outright, give the one it most plainly implies, in words
close to its own. Do not add numbers, dates, people, or events that it does not mention.

Keep names out of every part. Refer to people and organizations with generic labels such as
"the team", "the vendor", or "the customer", never by personal, company, or product names.

The causal chain matters most. List its links in the order they happen, each one leading to the
next, because an analogy with the right pieces in the wrong order teaches the listener the wrong
lesson. Use the fewest links that carry the cause and effect, usually three to five, and fold
together steps a listener would not miss. State the decision as a choice someone can make, and
the point as the one sentence the speaker wants the listener to walk away with.
